{
  "gap_analysis": "The source text defines early stopping as using validation performance to decide when to end training, contrasting this with using held-out test results. However, it does not specify the precise mechanism (e.g., monitoring for a plateau or increase in validation loss) that triggers the stop decision. To explain the 'how' of the strategy without inventing case-specific thresholds, I must introduce general knowledge about the relationship between training loss and validation loss curves, specifically the point where validation performance degrades while training continues to improve (the overfitting onset). This connects the abstract concept of 'validation performance' to the concrete action of 'ending training'.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "intended_fact": "Early stopping terminates training when validation performance stops improving or begins to degrade.",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "reason": "General machine learning knowledge dictates that early stopping is triggered by the divergence between training and validation metrics; specifically, when validation loss increases (or accuracy plateaus/drops) despite continued training improvements, indicating overfitting.",
      "gap_ids": [
        "question_0013"
      ],
      "added_knowledge": "The specific trigger condition: degradation or stagnation of the validation metric."
    },
    {
      "intended_fact": "Degradation of validation performance indicates that the model is overfitting to the training data.",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "reason": "This is a fundamental definition in machine learning: if a model performs worse on unseen validation data while improving on training data, it has memorized noise rather than learning generalizable patterns.",
      "gap_ids": [
        "question_0013"
      ],
      "added_knowledge": "The causal link between worsening validation metrics and the phenomenon of overfitting."
    }
  ],
  "reason": "The seed concept 'early stopping' is defined in the text as using validation performance to stop training. The gap asks for the specific trigger. General knowledge fills this by stating that the trigger is the point where validation performance ceases to improve (or worsens), which signifies overfitting. This connects the abstract 'validation performance' mentioned in the text to the concrete mechanism of detecting overfitting, answering the gap without contradicting the source or inventing specific numerical thresholds not present in the general theory."
